WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

1
00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.170
In this lesson, you will build your first coding agent from scratch.

2
00:00:04.771 --> 00:00:06.773
You'll give it real tools to execute code,

3
00:00:07.173 --> 00:00:10.777
read and write files and carry out multi-step reasoning loops.

4
00:00:10.977 --> 00:00:12.779
All right, let's get coding.

5
00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:16.783
Let's start by ignoring warnings with these two lines of code.

6
00:00:17.517 --> 00:00:19.252
All of the keys are set

7
00:00:19.252 --> 00:00:21.321
up for you on a DeepLearning.AI environment

8
00:00:21.521 --> 00:00:23.523
and you don't need to worry about it.

9
00:00:23.623 --> 00:00:25.592
Before we build our first agent,

10
00:00:25.692 --> 00:00:28.395
let's have a look at a more complex data analysis agent

11
00:00:28.495 --> 00:00:30.296
that you will build in the next...

12
00:00:30.363 --> 00:00:33.833
a taste of what you will be learning in this lesson.

13
00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:44.144
This agent is capable of running and executing code.

14
00:00:44.344 --> 00:00:48.848
So let's briefly just try one prompt to give you

15
00:00:49.082 --> 00:00:51.384
an idea of what we will be building.

16
00:00:51.684 --> 00:00:54.988
So let's ask, can you create me

17
00:00:55.088 --> 00:00:57.090
a function that draw

18
00:00:57.624 --> 00:01:01.027
an emoji and run it.

19
00:01:03.163 --> 00:01:06.266
On the right side, we see our context growing

20
00:01:06.966 --> 00:01:10.270
and we are using the same color scheme as in the slides,

21
00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:12.972
and the height is proportional to the token size.

22
00:01:13.206 --> 00:01:15.708
Here, the agent runs some code,

23
00:01:15.708 --> 00:01:17.510
it generates the code and runs it.

24
00:01:17.710 --> 00:01:20.113
looks like it's uh, yeah,

25
00:01:20.113 --> 00:01:23.016
it's making some Python code

26
00:01:23.016 --> 00:01:25.018
with matplotlib functions.

27
00:01:25.652 --> 00:01:28.521
but it's not really, it is not making a smiling emoji

28
00:01:28.621 --> 00:01:32.225
but for some reason a sad one, which is kind of interesting.

29
00:01:32.425 --> 00:01:33.927
And feel free to prompt it

30
00:01:33.927 --> 00:01:35.628
more and play with it as well.

31
00:01:36.129 --> 00:01:38.231
All right, back to our first coding agent.

32
00:01:38.631 --> 00:01:40.733
The first thing we need is a language model.

33
00:01:41.234 --> 00:01:45.638
For all of our lessons, we'll be using the OpenAI GPT models.

34
00:01:46.239 --> 00:01:49.442
We'll call the models by using a handy LLM function.

35
00:01:50.076 --> 00:01:52.278
taking an OpenAI client,

36
00:01:52.679 --> 00:01:54.881
a list of messages, a system prompt,

37
00:01:55.248 --> 00:01:58.551
and printing back the reply from the model.

38
00:02:00.854 --> 00:02:03.056
Next, we need to give our agent tools,

39
00:02:03.556 --> 00:02:06.559
actual function it can call to run things.

40
00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:09.262
Here we are defining a TypedDict

41
00:02:09.562 --> 00:02:13.066
that stores the results and errors, if any.

42
00:02:13.566 --> 00:02:15.568
Then the function execute_code

43
00:02:15.869 --> 00:02:19.072
runs whatever Python code is passed as a string.

44
00:02:19.405 --> 00:02:23.209
is using the exec built-in Python function to do so,

45
00:02:23.209 --> 00:02:26.913
and we also redirect standard output,

46
00:02:26.913 --> 00:02:30.216
so we can capture it and return it to us.

47
00:02:30.817 --> 00:02:32.719
Let's test it by printing Hello World.

48
00:02:32.719 --> 00:02:35.121
And we can see we have Hello World in our

49
00:02:35.121 --> 00:02:38.324
results key in the execution dictionary.

50
00:02:38.925 --> 00:02:41.294
In order for the agent to run this function,

51
00:02:41.294 --> 00:02:43.596
we need to describe it as a JSON schema.

52
00:02:44.297 --> 00:02:46.299
Note the name is execute_code

53
00:02:46.299 --> 00:02:50.904
and the parameters expect one property, code, as a string.

54
00:02:51.104 --> 00:02:54.607
We can register the tool by adding to the tools dictionary.

55
00:02:54.908 --> 00:02:57.610
Every tool we create later will go here as well.

56
00:03:04.584 --> 00:03:07.487
Next, we define a function called execute_tool.

57
00:03:08.288 --> 00:03:12.292
It takes the tool's name, finding the tools in a dictionary,

58
00:03:12.592 --> 00:03:16.796
pass the arguments and return the result.

59
00:03:17.197 --> 00:03:19.365
We also added some error handling

60
00:03:19.365 --> 00:03:22.769
in the case the agent had an error by parsing the arguments

61
00:03:22.769 --> 00:03:25.271
or calls a tool that does not exist.

62
00:03:26.339 --> 00:03:28.675
Now we can create our agent function.

63
00:03:28.975 --> 00:03:31.144
This is where the agent is going to live.

64
00:03:31.644 --> 00:03:35.748
We are passing the OpenAI client, an user query, system prompt,

65
00:03:36.049 --> 00:03:38.952
the tools registry, and the tools schemas.

66
00:03:39.152 --> 00:03:40.954
Similar to what we did at the

67
00:03:40.954 --> 00:03:42.755
beginning, we are creating the messages array.

68
00:03:43.056 --> 00:03:45.558
We are calling the LLM function with

69
00:03:45.558 --> 00:03:48.061
all the parameters, and we are iterating,

70
00:03:48.127 --> 00:03:51.331
through the response. If the part.type is message,

71
00:03:51.331 --> 00:03:53.333
it means that the model wants to speak

72
00:03:53.333 --> 00:03:55.602
back to us, so we're just printing the part.content.

73
00:03:55.602 --> 00:03:59.005
If the part.type is function_call, we are getting the function name,

74
00:03:59.205 --> 00:04:02.308
do some printing and use our execute_tool function

75
00:04:02.308 --> 00:04:04.310
to get the result from the tool.

76
00:04:05.111 --> 00:04:07.614
We can test it by making the agent

77
00:04:07.614 --> 00:04:09.949
generate some Python code and run it.

78
00:04:10.350 --> 00:04:12.619
Here we have a task-specific system prompt

79
00:04:12.619 --> 00:04:15.955
where we're telling the agent that is a Senior Python programmer,

80
00:04:16.289 --> 00:04:19.025
and we are also explaining how to use the tools.

81
00:04:19.292 --> 00:04:21.995
So here we're saying you must always use the execute_code

82
00:04:21.995 --> 00:04:23.997
tool to run code.

83
00:04:23.997 --> 00:04:26.499
Finally, we are telling how to collect user's inputs,

84
00:04:26.499 --> 00:04:28.801
just by using the input python function.

85
00:04:28.801 --> 00:04:30.803
Our task is straightforward.

86
00:04:30.803 --> 00:04:33.506
We want to make a program that asks us

87
00:04:33.506 --> 00:04:36.509
how many cups of coffee we had today,

88
00:04:36.509 --> 00:04:38.811
and then convert it back to lines of code.

89
00:04:39.879 --> 00:04:43.483
So, today I had a lot of coffee, actually.

90
00:04:44.050 --> 00:04:45.918
So let's go with five. And it

91
00:04:45.918 --> 00:04:48.087
looks like I wrote 215 lines of code

92
00:04:48.087 --> 00:04:50.790
because caffeine equal productivity.

93
00:04:51.424 --> 00:04:53.626
This is a full round trip.

94
00:04:54.127 --> 00:04:56.229
The agent received the user's query,

95
00:04:56.329 --> 00:04:59.932
reason about it, and decide which tool to call.

96
00:05:00.433 --> 00:05:03.202
A coding agent needs to use the file system

97
00:05:03.303 --> 00:05:05.405
to read, write, and edit files.

98
00:05:05.805 --> 00:05:09.108
Let's implement two tools to read and write files.

99
00:05:09.409 --> 00:05:12.512
First we need the read_file_schema. The name is read_file,

100
00:05:12.612 --> 00:05:15.114
is taking a file_path as a string of course,

101
00:05:15.315 --> 00:05:18.518
limit and offset. So how many characters and from where

102
00:05:18.618 --> 00:05:21.421
the agent is allowed to read from from the file.

103
00:05:21.587 --> 00:05:24.791
This is very important because we can have very big files

104
00:05:25.191 --> 00:05:27.694
and giving the model the ability to pick up

105
00:05:27.994 --> 00:05:29.595
how much characters it want to read

106
00:05:29.896 --> 00:05:31.497
can keep the context clean.

107
00:05:31.998 --> 00:05:34.367
The write_file_schema it's very similar.

108
00:05:34.367 --> 00:05:38.171
We are passing a content string and a file_path as well.

109
00:05:38.171 --> 00:05:40.673
Then we need to implement the actual function

110
00:05:40.673 --> 00:05:43.276
will be running when the agent wants to call.

111
00:05:43.643 --> 00:05:47.747
these two tools. Let's go ahead and implement the read_file function.

112
00:05:48.247 --> 00:05:51.150
Notice we are creating a custom exception.

113
00:05:51.351 --> 00:05:56.351
This is helpful because we can avoid passing long error stack traces

114
00:05:57.223 --> 00:05:59.425
and just create a specific

115
00:05:59.992 --> 00:06:03.796
concise error feedback back to the model.

116
00:06:04.197 --> 00:06:07.500
For example, if the file is not there,

117
00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:09.769
we are going to raise a new ToolError

118
00:06:09.769 --> 00:06:13.172
telling the model exactly that the file does not exist.

119
00:06:13.573 --> 00:06:15.475
Next, we implement the write_file function.

120
00:06:15.775 --> 00:06:18.411
Similar, we are also doing error

121
00:06:18.411 --> 00:06:21.481
handling in the same way as before.

122
00:06:22.081 --> 00:06:24.517
Finally, we can update our tools

123
00:06:24.517 --> 00:06:27.387
registry by adding these two new tools.

124
00:06:27.787 --> 00:06:31.991
Let's give it a test by creating a working dir,

125
00:06:32.158 --> 00:06:34.060
having a new user query in which

126
00:06:34.060 --> 00:06:35.962
we task the agent to create a

127
00:06:36.362 --> 00:06:40.366
blank text.txt file and telling where the working dir

128
00:06:40.666 --> 00:06:44.170
is supposed to be. Notice that we are passing our

129
00:06:44.737 --> 00:06:46.539
new tools_schemas

130
00:06:46.539 --> 00:06:48.541
to the coding_agent function.

131
00:06:48.841 --> 00:06:51.210
We can see the agent used our write_file tools

132
00:06:51.511 --> 00:06:54.514
and it has written zero bytes to that file.

133
00:06:55.114 --> 00:06:57.216
Let's now test our error handling.

134
00:06:58.017 --> 00:06:59.652
We can task the agent to

135
00:06:59.652 --> 00:07:01.521
read a file that is not there

136
00:07:01.721 --> 00:07:03.823
and see what happens. We can

137
00:07:03.823 --> 00:07:06.259
see it use the read_file tools again,

138
00:07:06.359 --> 00:07:10.062
but this time we got an error as a dictionary.

139
00:07:10.263 --> 00:07:13.466
And this time we got an error as a structured

140
00:07:13.900 --> 00:07:16.903
dictionary. This will help the agent

141
00:07:16.903 --> 00:07:19.972
recover and try again.

142
00:07:20.306 --> 00:07:24.744
So let's try to task the agent with something slightly more complicated.

143
00:07:25.011 --> 00:07:27.113
Here we want the agent to create

144
00:07:27.113 --> 00:07:29.182
a file with file one inside it

145
00:07:29.182 --> 00:07:31.083
and then read it back to us,

146
00:07:31.784 --> 00:07:33.786
as we were expecting,

147
00:07:33.786 --> 00:07:36.289
the agent is calling the write_file tool,

148
00:07:36.289 --> 00:07:39.292
writing five bytes to file one,

149
00:07:39.292 --> 00:07:41.294
and then the read_file tool.

150
00:07:41.294 --> 00:07:44.797
Then we can see that the content is actually file one.

151
00:07:44.797 --> 00:07:48.301
So far, our agent can just do one turn.

152
00:07:48.301 --> 00:07:53.272
But a real agent should iteratively perform tasks and stop when complete.

153
00:07:53.539 --> 00:07:56.342
We need to create a loop and exit conditions.

154
00:07:56.843 --> 00:08:00.346
The simplest way to decide when to stop

155
00:08:00.346 --> 00:08:02.448
is when the agent doesn't call new functions

156
00:08:02.448 --> 00:08:05.051
or reaches the maximum step limit.

157
00:08:05.651 --> 00:08:07.353
Let's go over the implementation.

158
00:08:07.720 --> 00:08:10.022
We are adding a new loop

159
00:08:10.523 --> 00:08:12.625
in the coding_agent function

160
00:08:13.326 --> 00:08:17.029
till a parameter max_steps is reached,

161
00:08:17.129 --> 00:08:19.532
defaults to five, and

162
00:08:19.866 --> 00:08:23.736
if the agent doesn't want to do a new function call,

163
00:08:24.070 --> 00:08:25.938
it means that it doesn't think

164
00:08:25.938 --> 00:08:28.074
it need to use the available tools.

165
00:08:28.541 --> 00:08:31.110
So we're going to label the task as completed.

166
00:08:31.577 --> 00:08:34.380
We are also keeping a list of messages

167
00:08:34.780 --> 00:08:37.383
and we are appending to it the initial user message,

168
00:08:37.984 --> 00:08:42.088
every message part, and the result from the tool.

169
00:08:43.789 --> 00:08:47.693
Let's test it out here. We want the model

170
00:08:47.693 --> 00:08:49.896
to create a Caesar cipher function,

171
00:08:50.396 --> 00:08:51.731
then we want it to ask

172
00:08:51.731 --> 00:08:53.299
me for a message, for the shift.

173
00:08:53.499 --> 00:08:56.769
Then we want to run it, print the ciphered message,

174
00:08:56.869 --> 00:08:59.272
and save it to a secret.txt file.

175
00:08:59.472 --> 00:09:00.973
So quite a lot of step

176
00:09:00.973 --> 00:09:02.675
that the agent will have to do.

177
00:09:03.943 --> 00:09:07.246
Super. I'm going to put my name, so Francesco.

178
00:09:07.947 --> 00:09:11.217
And I'm going to shift it by three letters, the alphabet.

179
00:09:11.717 --> 00:09:13.719
The message is this set of characters,

180
00:09:13.819 --> 00:09:15.721
and the model is telling me that

181
00:09:15.721 --> 00:09:17.623
it has written it to secret.txt file.

182
00:09:17.723 --> 00:09:19.926
I don't see any write_file tool,

183
00:09:20.026 --> 00:09:22.028
so probably it used Python to write to the file.

184
00:09:22.228 --> 00:09:24.230
So let's cat this file

185
00:09:24.430 --> 00:09:26.232
and see if it's really there.

186
00:09:27.533 --> 00:09:31.037
And we can see actually the agent wrote the right

187
00:09:31.037 --> 00:09:34.040
encrypted message to our folder.

188
00:09:35.241 --> 00:09:37.243
Let's create a simple interface

189
00:09:37.243 --> 00:09:39.745
to interact with our coding agent.

190
00:09:40.446 --> 00:09:42.448
We can test it out by

191
00:09:42.515 --> 00:09:45.017
asking the agent to create

192
00:09:45.518 --> 00:09:48.521
an ASCII cow and print it back to us.

193
00:09:55.328 --> 00:09:59.332
It worked and we can see our cow as ASCII art.

194
00:09:59.532 --> 00:10:02.335
Feel free to continue the conversation with your agent.

195
00:10:02.435 --> 00:10:04.704
The agent we just built in this

196
00:10:04.704 --> 00:10:07.340
lab is running code locally on the notebook.

197
00:10:07.740 --> 00:10:11.344
In the next lesson, you'll learn more about code execution environments

198
00:10:11.744 --> 00:10:15.014
and which one is the best, depending on the use case.
